K7  Minimality: 1 and 2 points are impossible, 3 points suffice; every 2-cycle violates (5)
====================================================================================================
  A 1-point space has a fixed point.  A fixed-point-free map on 2 points is the swap a <-> b; at the
  pair (a,b) the distance vector is D^2 (1,0,0,1,1,1), so the WGP inequality reads al+de+ep+ze <= 0,
  while alt1 forces al+de+ep+ze >= (1-A)(al+ze+2min(be,0)) - 4 min(be,0) > 0 and alt2 forces
  al+de+ep+ze >= (1-A)(al+ep+2min(ga,0)) - 4 min(ga,0) > 0.  This uses only A < 1; lambda-mixing
  (Lemma 2.2) produces another coefficient vector obeying the same inequality at the same pair,
  so no lambda helps.  The same computation applies to any 2-cycle {a,b} in any metric space.
  sanity grid: none of the 117649 vectors in {-3..3}^6 passes both, for A = 1/2 and A = 99/100.
  With the true shortcut map (T(1) = 2), the pair (1,2) has distance vector (1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1): the 2-cycle lemma
  forbids every coefficient choice there (the paper's own table gives LHS = +1 > 0).  Hence the paper's
  redefinition T(1) := 1 is FORCED by condition (5).  3-point counterexamples exist (K6a,b).
  VERDICT K7: the least counterexample has exactly 3 points (a 3-cycle); by K6b it can be taken inside
  N with the paper's constants: {5,7,10} under 3n-1.

