K10 The corrected Theorem 2.1 is one-step decay, and Collatz violates it for EVERY coefficient choice
====================================================================================================
  Corrected Theorem 2.1. If for every p in the orbit, alt1_lambda(p,Tp) or alt2_lambda(Tp,p) holds, then
  d(Tp,T^2p)^2 <= A d(p,Tp)^2 along the orbit, hence d(T^n x,T^m x) <= A^(n/2)/(1-A^(1/2)) d(x,Tx).
  Converse at a single step: if d(Tp,T^2p)^2 <= A d(p,Tp)^2, the vector (B,0,0,-AB,0,0) at (p,Tp)
  satisfies WGP there and alt1 (with B).  So the corrected hypothesis IS one-step decay.
  Collatz, p < 5000: one-step decay (A = 1/2) fails exactly at the odd p >= 3, where the ratio is
  >= 16/9 > 1 > A for ANY A < 1; the Collatz table covers exactly the decay steps.  At p = 5 the
  corrected hypothesis would force 16 <= 9A < 9.  Hence NO coefficient functions and NO lambda make
  the corrected Theorem 2.1 apply to Collatz on (N,|x-y|) -- already at the single point p = 5.
  (3n-1, odd p < 20001: every up-step stretches consecutive distances by at least 3/2, so the
   corrected theorem fails at every 3n-1 up-step too.)

====================================================================================================
K11 Caristi: the one metric fixed-point principle that survives is equivalent to Collatz
====================================================================================================
  Caristi: if phi >= 0 (l.s.c.) with d(x,Tx) <= phi(x) - phi(Tx), then T has a fixed point.  On
  (N,|x-y|) with the paper's T such phi exists iff every orbit reaches 1; then phi = the orbit's total
  variation, sum over n < tau(x) of ceil(T^n x / 2), with equality d(x,Tx) = phi(x) - phi(Tx).
  verified for 2 <= x <= 100000: |T(n)-n| = ceil(n/2) and d(x,Tx) = phi(x) - phi(Tx); e.g. phi(27) = 20328, phi(97) = 20734.
  A Caristi argument therefore needs exactly the finiteness of phi, i.e. Collatz itself.

====================================================================================================
K12 Contraction metrics for T: |x-y| fails; some metric <=> no cycles (Bessaga); a discrete one <=> Collatz
====================================================================================================
  (i)  d = |x-y|: no contraction-type certificate (K5, K10).
  (ii) Bessaga's converse (1959; statement as in Wikipedia 'Banach fixed-point theorem', Converses): if
       every iterate T^n of a self-map of a set has a unique fixed point, then for every q in (0,1) some
       complete metric makes T a q-contraction.  Conversely a contraction's iterates have unique fixed
       points.  For the paper's T on N (T(1) = 1) this says: a Banach-contraction metric exists iff N has
       no T-cycle other than {1} -- the CYCLE half only; divergent orbits would still converge to 1 in it.
  (iii) A UNIFORMLY DISCRETE complete metric making T a contraction exists iff every orbit reaches 1:
       (=>) rho(T^n x, 1) <= q^n rho(x, 1) < inf rho forces T^n x = 1;  (<=) rho(x,y) = K^max(h(x),h(y))
       for x != y (h = steps to 1, K = 1/q > 1) is an ultrametric, >= 1 off the diagonal, and
       rho(Tx,Ty) = q rho(x,y) or 0.
  checked for 1 <= x, y <= 1500 (q = 1/2, heights from the actual orbits): rho >= 1 off the diagonal,
  rho(Tx,Ty) <= rho(x,y)/2, and the ultrametric inequality on 20000 random triples.
  So metric contraction reformulates Collatz exactly as 'the height h is finite everywhere'; the
  divergence half is precisely the uniform discreteness that the paper's last step takes for granted.

