====================================================================================================
I1  Lemma 2.1 = triangle inequality + AM-QM; what it discards
====================================================================================================
  Lemma 2.1: 2 min(theta,0) (a^2 + b^2) <= theta c^2, with a = d(x,z), b = d(z,y), c = d(x,y).
   * theta >= 0: it reads 0 <= theta c^2 -- the LOWER half of the sandwich, (a-b)^2 <= c^2, is thrown away;
   * theta < 0: it reads c^2 <= 2(a^2 + b^2), i.e. c^2 <= (a+b)^2 (triangle) and (a+b)^2 <= 2(a^2+b^2)
     (AM-QM, equivalently 2ab <= a^2 + b^2).  The cross term +-2ab is replaced by its sign-blind bound.
   * equality (theta < 0) iff c = a + b and a = b: z is a metric midpoint of x and y.
  Lemma 2.1 checked on 483480 (metric, triple, theta) instances: random 7-point path metrics, R^2 with l1, l2, l_inf.
  Collatz triples (p, Tp, T^2 p), 2 <= p <= 10^5: 50000 monotone (upper equality, Lemma 2.1 loses (d1-d2)^2), 49999 turning (lower equality, loses (d1+d2)^2).
  Sharpness needs a monotone triple with d1 = d2; d1 = d2 happens only at p = 2 (the turning triple
  2, 1, 2 of the 2-cycle), so Lemma 2.1 is never sharp along a positive Collatz orbit.  What it discards
  is the orientation (turn / no turn) of the orbit together with the size mismatch d1 - d2.

====================================================================================================
I2  On the line every orbit triple is an equality case; the cross-term sign is side-blind
====================================================================================================
  4240799 consecutive triples of orbits from [-2*10^4, 2*10^4] \ {0} (120 steps each):
  d(x_(n-1), x_(n+1)) = d1 + d2 exactly when x_(n-1), x_n have the same parity (cross term +2 d1 d2),
  and |d1 - d2| exactly when the parity changes (cross term -2 d1 d2) -- on BOTH half-lines.
  The rule depends only on the parity word, hence (Theorem 6 of the mod-192 note) it is side-blind.
  The 'central part' (cross term 0 with both steps nonzero) never occurs on the line: a zero step
  occurs only at a fixed point (here [-1]; 0 is the other one).  A strictly-inside
  triangle needs a second dimension, where the middle case is orthogonality (section I5).

====================================================================================================
I3  The sign law is the pair of equality cases of the sandwich, with z = 0 in the middle
====================================================================================================
  all words of length p <= 12, 6-7 lifts each (both signs): 57318 checks of
    2^p T^p(x) = 3^a x + c_w  and  |2^p T^p(x)| = 3^a|x| + sgn(x) c_w  (c_w >= 0),
  i.e. with X = 3^a x, Y = -c_w and z = 0:  x > 0 <=> 0 lies between X and Y <=> UPPER equality
  d(X,Y) = d(X,0) + d(0,Y) (cross term +2 3^a|x| c_w);  x < 0 <=> LOWER equality d(X,Y) = |d(X,0) - d(0,Y)|.
  Transport T_+^p(-x) = -T_-^p(x) turns the negative half-line of 3n+1 into the positive half-line of
  3n-1: the lower side of the sandwich IS the 3n-1 sheet.  The middle point of the owner's inequality
  is literally 0, and the sign law says on which side of it the orbit's two ingredients sit.

====================================================================================================
I4  The central sheet b = 0 (Mahler's multiplicative map) and the Mahler bridge
====================================================================================================
  for all 2046 words with p <= 10:  T_0^w = (T_+^w + T_-^w)/2 = odd part of T_+^w, and
  (T_b^w x)^2 = (9^a x^2 + 2b 3^a c_w x + b^2 c_w^2)/4^p: the cross term has sign b*sgn(x).  So the three
  sheets b = -1, 0, +1 are the left side, the centre and the right side of the owner's sandwich (x > 0).
  odd branch: T_b(x) + b = (3/2)(x + b) -- the central map x -> 3x/2 centred at the fixed point -b;
  the even branch x -> x/2 is centred at 0.  The b = 0 sheet is where both centres coincide.
  shadowing identity (exact, x in [-500,500], n <= 60, both sheets): T_b^n(x) = (3^a_n/2^n) xi_n with
  xi_n = x + b c_n/3^a_n; xi_n increases on the plus sheet and decreases on the minus sheet.  Every
  orbit point is the CENTRAL-sheet image of a shifted start; the shift is the Bernstein partial sum.
  THM-4469 instance (blocks 0111101110 / 1101100111, R = 4726 / 4727, cylinder classes 990 / 187 mod 1024,
  alpha = 2187/1024): for 40 random block sequences of length 6, realized by the least positive integer
  of their Terras class,
  x_j = xi alpha^j - t_j with t_j in [4726,4727]/1163 * (1 - rho^(6-j)) exactly: the +1 orbit is the
  central-sheet (Mahler) orbit xi alpha^j pushed DOWN by a confined carry; the 3n-1 orbit of -x is the
  central orbit of -xi pushed UP by the same carry.  This is the precise sense in which the Mahler bridge
  says '+-1-corner orbits are confined like central-sheet orbits'.

====================================================================================================
I5  Orthogonality in C: the Pythagorean middle, Chamberland's f and the LSW f_0
====================================================================================================
  |z + w|^2 = |z|^2 + |w|^2 + 2 Re(z conj w): the middle (Pythagorean) case is Re(z conj w) = 0.  For an
  affine branch L_w(z) = (3^a z + c_w)/2^p the cross term is 2 3^a c_w Re(z): right half-plane = '+2ab',
  left half-plane = '-2ab', imaginary axis = the orthogonal middle.  (20000 random checks.)
  Chamberland f(x) = x + 1/4 - ((2x+1)/4) cos(pi x): f = T on Z and f'(n) = 1/2 (even), 3/2 (odd) --
  f keeps the branch slopes, so it is conformal at integers (orthogonal stays orthogonal).
  LSW f_0(z) = z/2 + (1 - cos pi z)(z + 1/2)/2 + (1/2 - cos pi z) sin(pi z)/pi (h = 0, as printed in
  Chamberland's survey): f_0 = T on Z and f_0'(n) = 0 -- every integer is a critical point.  (n in [-30,30].)
  f_0'(x) = sin(pi x) (2 sin(pi x) + (pi/2)(x + 1/2)) (checked); its real zeros are Z together with
  -1.644421083, -1.179060746, -0.1013635846 (only |x + 1/2| <= 4/pi can solve the second factor).
  NOTE: Lagarias's annotated bibliography (entry 115) summarizes the real critical points of f_0 as
  'the integers together with -1/2'; for the formula as printed, f_0'(-1/2) = 2, so this could not be
  reconciled (primary source not read: publisher bot-check).  Nothing below depends on it.
  At each critical integer n, f_0(n + y) - T(n) ~ c_n y^2 and f_0(n + iy) - T(n) ~ -c_n y^2 (c_n real,
  checked at n = -5,-2,1,2,7): the fold sends the ORTHOGONAL direction onto the OPPOSITE real ray.  This is
  the exact content of 'the corner at 0 introducing orthogonality': squaring (the smooth version of |x|)
  identifies +-x and turns the orthogonal axis into the sign.
  Stability consequence: in Chamberland's f an integer cycle has multiplier 3^a/2^p, so (sign law) it is
  attracting iff its points are >= 0; in LSW's f_h every integer cycle is superattracting on BOTH sides.

====================================================================================================
I6  |T(x)| = T_sgn(x)(|x|); linear pair sums, quadratic pair products, and the cross term
====================================================================================================
  |T_+(x)| = T_sgn(x)(|x|) for all |x| <= 10^6 (T_0(0) = 0), and iterated for p <= 50, |x| <= 10^4.
  On the size coordinate |x| the single map T_+ becomes the PAIR T_+1, T_-1, selected by sgn x = d|x|/dx:
  on odd x, |T(x)| = (3|x| + sgn x)/2.  The corner of |x| at 0 is exactly where the selector jumps.
  THM-4470 pairs {2i-1, 2i} -> {3i-1, i}, i <= 10^6:  (a+b) conserved (LINEAR invariant),
  ab scaled by 3/4 + 1/(8i-4) (QUADRATIC contraction), and exactly  D(2ab) = -2i(i-1) = -D(a^2+b^2):
  in (a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2 the left side is fixed and T moves mass from the cross term to the squares.
  Equivalently QM^2 + GM^2 = 2 AM^2 with AM fixed: T lowers GM and raises QM by the same amount.  Lemma 2.1
  keeps only QM >= AM; the Collatz drift log(2/sqrt3) (the AM-GM gap) lives in the discarded cross term.
  Even observables (|x|, x^2, |x - y|) are invariant under x -> -x, hence side-blind (Theorem 6 of the
  mod-192 note); only odd observables (x, sgn x, ...) can carry the sign law.

====================================================================================================
I7  The fixed points {0, -b}: the centres of the two branches; the self-mapped pairs
====================================================================================================
  Fix(T_b) on Z is {0, -b} (checked on [-10^5, 10^5]); D(0) = 0 and E_b(-b) = -b are the Banach fixed
  points of the one-letter inverse words (chains script F1); E_b(x) + b = (2/3)(x + b).
  Consecutive pairs of 3n+1, {2i-1, 2i}, mapped onto themselves (|i| <= 10^5): i = 0, the pair {-1, 0}
  of the two fixed points (pointwise), and i = 1, {1, 2} (swapped: the 2-cycle).  For 3n-1, {2i, 2i+1}:
  {0, 1} (pointwise) and {-2, -1} (swapped) -- the mirror images.  The 'central pair' straddling the
  sign is {-1, 0} for 3n+1 and {0, 1} for 3n-1: b decides on which side of 0 the odd branch is centred.

