====================================================================================================
F3  Banach in R and in Z_3: the sign law decides which words are real contractions
====================================================================================================
  words of length <= 10 with a >= 1: 411 inverse maps are real contractions (slope 2^p/3^a < 1),
  exactly those with x_w < 0; 1625 are real expansions, exactly those with x_w > 0.
  backward (inverse-word) iteration in R converges to -1 (slope 2/3), -5 (8/9) and -17 (2048/2187),
  and is repelled by 1 (word 10, slope 4/3).  So Banach in R sees exactly the NEGATIVE cycles; the
  positive ones are forward-attracting.  In Z_2 every word contracts (factor 2^-p): Banach sees all.
  In Z_3, D is an isometry and E expands by 3: no contraction principle at the prime 3, where
  integrality (the gate (2^p - 3^a) | c_w and the legality x = 2 mod 3) is decided.

====================================================================================================
F4  Brouwer (IVT) for continuous extensions: Chamberland's f, Sharkovskii, covering chains
====================================================================================================
  f(x) = (x/2) cos^2(pi x/2) + ((3x+1)/2) sin^2(pi x/2) = x + 1/4 - ((2x+1)/4) cos(pi x)  [Chamberland 1996]:
  f(n) = T(n) exactly for |n| <= 10^4, and f'(n) = 1 - (-1)^n/2 in {1/2, 3/2}.
    {0}            period  1  multiplier 1/2        attracting
    {1,2}          period  2  multiplier 3/4        attracting
    {-1}           period  1  multiplier 3/2        repelling
    {-5,-7,-10}    period  3  multiplier 9/8        repelling
    {-17,...}      period 11  multiplier 2187/2048  repelling
  An integer cycle of T is an f-cycle with multiplier 3^a/2^p; by the sign law it is attracting iff its
  points are >= 0.  So in Chamberland's extension the SIGN decides the STABILITY of integer cycles.
  Chamberland's numbers re-derived: mu1 = 0.277733766172, mu2 = 1.57737324444, mu3 = 2.44570769366;
  second attracting 2-cycle A2 = {1.19253190705, 2.13865633552} (multiplier -0.230753);
  f([mu1, mu3]) inside [mu1, mu3] on a 4*10^5-point grid (range [0.2777, 2.4457]).
  These match the values cited from Chamberland 1996 via Lagarias's bibliography (entry 35) and
  Chamberland's 2003 survey (section 6.5).
  positive side, J0=[2,3], J1=[3,4] (full 2-shift; f(J0), f(J1) contain J0 u J1):
    periodic orbits constructed by the IVT chain, one per admissible Lyndon itinerary, periods 1..7: [2, 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18]
  negative side, I0=[-10,-7], I1=[-7,-5] from the 3-cycle (f(I0) contains I0 u I1, f(I1) contains I0):
    periodic orbits constructed by the IVT chain, one per admissible Lyndon itinerary, periods 1..7: [1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 4]
  negative side, [-4,-3], [-3,-2] (full 2-shift, like the positive side):
    periodic orbits constructed by the IVT chain, one per admissible Lyndon itinerary, periods 1..7: [2, 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18]
  So every continuous extension of T has periodic points of EVERY period on the positive side (from the
  three values T(2)=1, T(3)=5, T(4)=2: Block-Coppel turbulence) and on the negative side (from the 3-cycle
  {-5,-7,-10} via Sharkovskii/Li-Yorke, and also from T(-4)=-2, T(-3)=-4, T(-2)=-1).  The sign does NOT
  decide the Sharkovskii type of f; it decides only which INTEGER cycles exist (positive: periods 1, 2;
  negative: 1, 3, 11, for p <= 24 by F2) and, in Chamberland's f, their stability.
  Growth of the real chain: at least 2^n points of period dividing n in [2,4] (one per itinerary), the
  same 2^n as the Banach count in Z_2 (F1).
  monotone divergent orbit (Chamberland; LSW: a Cantor set of them): f([2j+2,2j+3]) contains [2j+4,2j+5]
  for all j >= 0, so nested compact pullbacks give x0 = 2.88652508471819 with f^j(x0) in [2j+2, 2j+3],
  j = 0..13 (checked); by compactness some point does this for all j.  Divergence of a real extension
  is not Collatz divergence: it lives between the integers.

====================================================================================================
F5  Parity principles: Sperner, Redei, and the Banach count
====================================================================================================
  Sperner: 3000 random 1D and 400 random 2D Sperner labellings -- the number of fully labelled cells is
  always odd (the combinatorial core of Brouwer).
  Redei: all 33867 labelled tournaments on n <= 6 vertices have an odd number of Hamiltonian paths;
  n = 6 values: [1, 3, 5, 9, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19, 23, 25, 27, 29, 31, 33, 37, 41, 43, 45].
  Common mechanism: |S| = |Fix(i)| mod 2 for an involution i (door-to-door pairing for Sperner; arc-flip
  or OCF parity for Redei, repo THM-001/THM-002).  For Collatz the corresponding count is not a parity:
  each word has EXACTLY ONE 2-adic fixed point (F1), and the whole difficulty is integrality at the gate
  (2^p - 3^a) | c_w, a congruence modulo an odd number prime to 6 -- invisible to every mod-2 count.

